subdominer
----------

Passive subdomain discovery from certificate transparency logs, DNS
intelligence, historical datasets, and external security intelligence sources.

subdominer queries multiple external providers to discover hostnames associated
with a target domain. Results from the providers are normalized, aggregated,
and emitted as JSON Lines (JSONL).

The tool does not perform active DNS brute-forcing, port scanning, or direct
probing of discovered hosts. It relies on information already available from
external data sources.


Providers

    Providers run automatically.

        - Cert Spotter (subdominer)
        - Common Crawl
        - crt.name
        - crt.sh
        - AlienVault OTX
        - ProjectDiscovery Chaos
        - Shodan CT
        - Shodan DNS
        - urlscan.io
        - VirusTotal
        - Wayback Machine


API keys


    Configured through environment variables.

        CERTSPOTTER_API_KEY
            Optional.  Used when querying Cert Spotter.

        OTX_API_KEY
            Optional.  Used when querying AlienVault OTX.

        PDCP_API_KEY
            Required for ProjectDiscovery Chaos.  Skipped if key is missing.

        SHODAN_API_KEY
            Required for Shodan DNS.  Skipped if key is missing.

        URLSCAN_API_KEY
            Optional.  Used when querying urlscan.io.

        VIRUSTOTAL_API_KEY
            Required for VirusTotal.  Skipped if key is missing.


    The following providers do not require API keys:

        - Common Crawl
        - crt.name
        - crt.sh
        - Shodan CT
        - Wayback Machine


Usage

    subdominer [options] [domain ...]


Options

    -file <path>
        Read target domains from a file, one domain per line.

    -log <path>
        Write provider logs to the specified file.
        Defaults to ./subdominer.log.

    -silent
        Disable application logging.


    If neither positional domains nor -file is supplied, subdominer reads
    domains from stdin.


Examples

    subdominer example.com

    subdominer example.com example.org

    subdominer -file domains.txt

    printf '%s\n' example.com example.org | subdominer

    subdominer -silent example.com

    subdominer example.com > results.jsonl


Input

    Domains can be supplied in three ways:

        - As positional command-line arguments.
        - Through a file specified with -file.
        - Through stdin.

    Input files and stdin accept one domain per line.  Empty lines are ignored.

    The -file option cannot be combined with positional domains.

    Domains are normalized before processing.  Leading and trailing whitespace
    and a trailing dot are removed, and domain names are converted to lowercase.
    Invalid domain names cause the application to exit with an error.


Output

    Results are written as JSON Lines (JSONL) to stdout. Each line represents
    one unique URL for a target domain.

    Each record contains:

        - schema_version
        - domain
        - url
        - evidence

    Logs are written to file, separately from the JSONL results, so that stdout
    can be redirected or piped into other tools.
